ild and runtime configuration | dependency-signal |\n210\t| crypto-build-signal | OpenSSL | Build file references OpenSSL; presence does not establish that application TLS uses a FIPS-validated provider | dependency-signal |\n211\t| fips-posture | FIPS validation | FIPS validation and runtime provider selection are not fully determined by static dependency/build signals | not-extracted |\n212\t\n213\t### FIPS Compliance\n214\t\n215\t#### Build-Time FIPS\n216\t\n217\t| Aspect | Value | Source |\n218\t|--------|-------|--------|\n219\t| **Build flags** | N/A (Rust, not Go) | Cargo.toml:109-112 |\n220\t| **Linking** | Dynamic (Rust default with system OpenSSL for protobuf build) | Dockerfile.konflux:38 |\n221\t| **OpenSSL in image** | Build stage only; not installed in release image | Dockerfile.konflux:17, 51-53 |\n222\t| **OLM FIPS annotation** | Not present (not an operator) | N/A |\n223\t\n224\t#### Application-Level Crypto\n225\t\n226\t| Aspect | Value | Source |\n227\t|--------|-------|--------|\n228\t| **TLS configuration** | rustls with ring provider; optional server TLS and mTLS via CLI flags | Cargo.toml:62-65, src/args.rs:43-47 |\n229\t| **Crypto libraries** | rustls 0.23.36 (ring), hyper-rustls 0.27.7 (ring), tokio-rustls 0.26.4 (ring), tonic (tls-ring) | Cargo.toml:33-34, 62-65, 81-83, 85-88 |\n230\t| **Certificate handling** | File-based certs/keys via CLI flags; per-service TLS configs in YAML | src/args.rs:43-47, config/config.yaml:53-66 |\n231\t| **Non-FIPS crypto risks** | ring is explicitly selected as the cryptographic provider across all TLS dependencies; ring is not a FIPS-validated provider | Cargo.toml:34, 63, 82, 86 |\n232\t\n233\tThe orchestrator explicitly selects `ring` as its cryptographic provider through Cargo.toml feature flags on rustls (`features = [\"ring\", \"std\"]`), hyper-rustls (`features = [\"ring\", ...]`), tokio-rustls (`features = [\"ring\"]`), and tonic (`features = [\"tls-ring\", ...]`). Ring is not a FIPS-validated cryptographic provider. OpenSSL is installed in the build stage for protobuf source compilation (`protobuf-src`) but is not present in the release image. The service cannot claim FIPS compliance for its TLS operations. [source: Cargo.toml:33-34, 62-65, 81-83, 85-88, 99-102, Dockerfile.konflux:17, 51-53]\n234\t## Data Flows\n235\t\n236\t### Flow 1: Text Generation with Guardrails\n237\t\n238\t| Step | Source | Destination | Port | Protocol | Encryption | Auth |\n239\t|------|--------|-------------|------|----------|------------|------|\n240\t| 1 | Client | guardrails-server | 8033/TCP | HTTP/HTTPS | TLS 1.2+ (optional) | Passthrough headers |\n241\t| 2 | guardrails-server | Chunker services | 8085/TCP | gRPC | TLS (optional, configurable) | None |\n242\t| 3 | guardrails-server | Detector services | 8080/TCP | HTTP/HTTPS | TLS (optional, configurable) | Bearer token (optional) |\n243\t| 4 | guardrails-server | TGIS generation service | 8033/TCP | gRPC | None | None |\n244\t| 5 | guardrails-server | Client | 8033/TCP | HTTP/HTTPS | TLS 1.2+ (optional) | N/A |\n245\t\n246\tThe orchestrator receives client requests on the v1/v2 REST API surface, chunks input text via gRPC chunker services, sends chunks to detector services for content safety analysis, and coordinates text generation through the TGIS gRPC backend. Configured client headers are forwarded to downstream services; X-Forwarded-Access-Token headers can optionally be rewritten to Authorization Bearer for downstream authentication. [source: src/server/routes.rs:70-108, 452-480, config/config.yaml:6-46]\n247\t\n248\t### Flow 2: Health Monitoring\n249\t\n250\t| Step | Source | Destination | Port | Protocol | Encryption | Auth |\n251\t|------|--------|-------------|------|----------|------------|------|\n252\t| 1 | Platform / Probe | health-server | 8034/TCP | HTTP | None | None |\n253\t| 2 | guardrails-server | Detector health services | 8081/TCP | HTTP/HTTPS | None | None |\n254\t\n255\tThe health server exposes unauthenticated `/health` and `/info` endpoints on a separate port. Optionally, on startup the orchestrator can probe detector health endpoints to verify downstream service availability. [source: src/server/routes.rs:62-66, src/args.rs:48-49, config/config.yaml:42-44]\n256\t\n257\t## Integration Points\n258\t\n259\t- **Chunker services:** gRPC client; protocol: gRPC; port: 8085; purpose: Configured downstream grpc client. [source: Cargo.toml:1, 17-33, 41-42, 47-49, 53-56, 66-68, 70-73, 81, 84-85, 90-97, config/config.yaml:12, 26, 38, 43, src/args.rs:32, 34, 43, 47]\n260\t- **Detector health services:** REST health client; protocol: HTTP/HTTPS; port: 8081; purpose: Configured downstream rest health client. [source: Cargo.toml:1, 17-33, 41-42, 47-49, 53-56, 66-68, 70-73, 81, 84-85, 90-97, config/config.yaml:12, 26, 38, 43, src/args.rs:32, 34, 43, 47]\n261\t- **Detector services:** REST API client; protocol: HTTP/HTTPS; port: 8080; purpose: Configured downstream rest api client. [source: Cargo.toml:1, 17-33, 41-42, 47-49, 53-56, 66-68, 70-73, 81, 84-85, 90-97, config/config.yaml:12, 26, 38, 43, src/args.rs:32, 34, 43, 47]\n262\t- **TGIS generation service:** gRPC client; protocol: gRPC; port: 8033; purpose: Configured downstream grpc client. [source: Cargo.toml:1, 17-33, 41-42, 47-49, 53-56, 66-68, 70-73, 81, 84-85, 90-97, config/config.yaml:12, 26, 38, 43, src/args.rs:32, 34, 43, 47]\n263\t\n264\t| Component | Interaction Type | Role | Port | Protocol | Encryption | Purpose |\n265\t|---------|----------------|----|----|--------|----------|-------|\n266\t| Chunker services | gRPC client |  | 8085 | gRPC | TLS (optional, configurable) | Configured downstream grpc client |\n267\t| Detector health services | REST health client |  | 8081 | HTTP/HTTPS | None | Configured downstream rest health client |\n268\t| Detector services | REST API client |  | 8080 | HTTP/HTTPS | TLS (optional, configurable) | Configured downstream rest api client |\n269\t| TGIS generation service | gRPC cli