{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-432a4b13-97a5-445f-b710-e939aaab9bb6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-852c9010d13b4919","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8001-n6lpo8ou/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8001-E002.md","content":"# Investigate ML-KEM TLS migration feasibility for fms-guardrails-orchestrator\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation determines whether the fms-guardrails-orchestrator service — whose TLS stack is rustls with the ring provider across its inbound HTTPS surface and downstream gRPC/REST clients, and which therefore cannot inherit PQC from a base image swap — can be migrated to an ML-KEM (FIPS 203) capable TLS configuration in time for the September 15, 2026 deadline. It tracks TLS migration spike RHOAIENG-7452. The outcome decides whether the guardrails TLS migration proceeds or the team files an OCP exception documenting the TLS stack, why it cannot negotiate ML-KEM, and the migration as the remediation plan.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Identify a rustls provider configuration (for example, an ML-KEM-capable crypto provider and the crate versions that enable X25519MLKEM768 hybrid key exchange) applicable to the orchestrator's dependency set: rustls, hyper-rustls, tokio-rustls, and tonic TLS.\n- Verify, ideally with a local build and integration test, that the candidate configuration negotiates ML-KEM hybrid key exchange while remaining backwards-compatible with classic ECDHE TLS 1.3 clients.\n- Estimate the migration effort (dependency and feature-flag changes, test coverage, review and release) against the September 15 deadline.\n- Record a recommendation: migrate now, or file an OCP exception via OCPEXCEPT with the migration as the documented remediation plan.\n- Hand findings to the exception filing if that path is chosen, so the filing states the component's TLS stack and why it cannot negotiate ML-KEM, as the strategy's acceptance criteria require.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A documented answer to: which provider/library configuration (with versions) enables ML-KEM hybrid key exchange for the orchestrator's inbound TLS and downstream TLS clients, or a documented conclusion that none is viable.\n- A verified demonstration (local build/integration test) of X25519MLKEM768 hybrid negotiation with a PQC-capable peer and continued interoperability with a non-PQC TLS 1.3 client, where a viable configuration exists.\n- An effort estimate and timeline for the migration compared against the September 15, 2026 deadline.\n- A recorded recommendation (migrate vs. OCP exception with remediation plan) usable directly for the compliance decision.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-2 (P0): the feasibility outcome is the decision input for the guardrails team's exception filing (or avoidance of one) by September 15.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":77555,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b1da33aad9f5aea0","agent_id":"a1b9c8d3f7c4bce05","session_id":"2c0c1fc8-055a-4722-8bad-6dca3da0d413","uuid":"81575377-353b-44a5-a98a-66246d69151c","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:10:49.312Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8001"}
