{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-462c7ded-d040-481e-954f-0c35698c6ef4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-926b420fe13dc220","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8001-n6lpo8ou/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8001-E010.md","content":"# PQC compliance verification CI scan across the RHOAI payload\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic builds the automated CI scan that measures the strategy's primary acceptance criterion: across the RHOAI 3.6 payload, every OpenSSL-backed container image rebuilt on a PQC-enabled base reports `DEFAULT:PQ` as its active crypto policy and exposes the ML-KEM algorithms in system OpenSSL. The scan produces the per-image pass/fail evidence that feeds the aggregated compliance view and closes the loop on the base image switch campaigns. It is distinct from the TLS-scanner CI owned by the central TLS profile strategy, which remains out of scope here.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement a CI scan job that, for each image in the RHOAI 3.6 payload list (sourced from the RHOAI-Build-Config CSV relatedImages inventory), executes `update-crypto-policies --show` and `openssl list -kem-algorithms` inside the container and records `DEFAULT:PQ` and the presence of ML-KEM-512, ML-KEM-768, and ML-KEM-1024.\n- Produce a per-image pass/fail report aggregated across the payload, consumable by the compliance tracking view.\n- Handle exemptions: images covered by filed and acknowledged OCP exceptions (non-OpenSSL TLS stacks such as rustls+ring and Java/NSS) are reported as exception-covered, not failed.\n- Document the FIPS-mode caveat: on FIPS-mode clusters, FIPS policy deactivates PQC for this release — expected behavior, not a compliance failure for RHOAI 3.6 — so the scan does not flag FIPS-cluster results as failures.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The scan executes against the full RHOAI 3.6 payload image list and emits a per-image pass/fail for the `DEFAULT:PQ` policy check and the ML-KEM algorithm availability check.\n- Images with acknowledged OCP exceptions are reported as exception-covered rather than failed, so the report distinguishes compliant, non-compliant, and exception-covered images.\n- Scan results are consumed by the aggregated compliance view, satisfying the strategy's \"measured by automated CI scan across the RHOAI 3.6 payload\" clause.\n- The FIPS-mode exemption is documented in the scan's reporting so FIPS-cluster behavior is not counted as a compliance failure.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): provides the automated verification that all RHOAI runtime images use a PQC-enabled base image (or are exception-covered).\n- HLR-3 (P0): the scan's per-image checks confirm the runtime-only switch took effect in the final image (policy active in the runtime container).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":83323,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b1da33aad9f5aea0","agent_id":"a1b9c8d3f7c4bce05","session_id":"2c0c1fc8-055a-4722-8bad-6dca3da0d413","uuid":"8579139e-243a-48be-ab43-0cbbc5d83320","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:12:19.790Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8001"}
