{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ac110c8ae0fbccfe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8001-E003\n3\ttitle: Audit trainer data cache Rust TLS posture\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n5\tcomponent: Training Kubeflow\n6\tteam: Training\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tinvestigation_signals:\n10\t  question_specificity: 1\n11\t  source_accessibility: 1\n12\t  local_runnability: 1\n13\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n14\t  human_judgment_required: 0\n15\timplementation_type: null\n16\tdependencies: []\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: High\n19\tai_implementability_score: 3\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Audit trainer data cache Rust TLS posture\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tThis investigation resolves the strategy's unknown TLS posture of the trainer data cache: whether it uses system OpenSSL (via native-tls or an equivalent OpenSSL-backed path), in which case the PQC base image switch alone confers `DEFAULT:PQ` and ML-KEM support, or a pure-Rust TLS stack (rustls with the ring provider), in which case it cannot inherit PQC from a base image and needs a TLS migration or an OCP exception. It tracks audit spike RHOAIENG-7196.\n30\t\n31\t## Scope\n32\t\n33\t- Inspect the trainer data cache source and dependency lockfiles to identify its TLS library and crypto provider for both inbound and outbound connections.\n34\t- Determine, from the identified stack, whether the PQC base image switch is sufficient for ML-KEM hybrid key exchange support or whether a provider migration is required.\n35\t- If a pure-Rust stack is found: assess the migration path (ML-KEM-capable provider), estimate effort against the September 15, 2026 deadline, and recommend migrate vs. OCP exception via OCPEXCEPT with the migration as remediation.\n36\t- Record the finding in the compliance tracking structure so the trainer data cache image is classified correctly (switch-covered vs. exception/migration path).\n37\t\n38\t## Acceptance Criteria\n39\t\n40\t- The trainer data cache's TLS library and crypto provider are documented, with source references (dependency manifests/lockfiles).\n41\t- A recorded determination: PQC base image switch sufficient (OpenSSL-backed), or ML-KEM-capable TLS migration required (pure-Rust stack).\n42\t- If migration is required: an effort estimate against the September 15, 2026 deadline and a recommendation (migrate vs. OCP exception with remediation plan).\n43\t\n44\t## HLR Traceability\n45\t\n46\t- HLR-1 (P0): determines whether the trainer data cache image is covered by the PQC base image switch or needs separate remediation.\n47\t- HLR-2 (P0): the posture outcome is the decision input for the trainer team's exception filing (or avoidance of one) by September 15.\n48\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a7f46cb863dca2e5","agent_id":"a8e80f1fe8fe243da","session_id":"2c0c1fc8-055a-4722-8bad-6dca3da0d413","uuid":"7e925d8c-9342-4b97-80f5-c55bba871d12","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:15:30.309Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ac110c8ae0fbccfe","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
