{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8abb39f1453a1148","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8001-E004\n3\ttitle: PQC Readiness Matrix and aggregated compliance tracking\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n5\tcomponent: Build and Release\n6\tteam: DevOps/Build\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tai_signals:\n10\t  change_specificity: 0\n11\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n12\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n13\t  existing_foundation: 1\n14\t  open_questions: 1\n15\t  external_dependency: 0\n16\t  human_process_gates: -1\n17\t  repo_access: 1\n18\t  architecture_claims: 1\n19\timplementation_type: null\n20\tdependencies: []\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 3\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# PQC Readiness Matrix and aggregated compliance tracking\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic creates and drives the strategy's central coordination deliverables: the PQC Readiness Matrix — a per-image classification of every RHOAI runtime image (current base, target PQC base or exception, TLS stack, owning team, status) — and the aggregated compliance view that gives OCP 5.0 visibility into the September 15, 2026 deadline. It ensures every image is accounted for as switched, in progress via a linked team tracking ticket, or covered by a filed OCP exception, and it drives the exception process for teams that cannot comply.\n34\t\n35\t## Scope\n36\t\n37\t- Build the PQC Readiness Matrix per component from the RHOAI-Build-Config rhoai-3.5 CSV (153 relatedImages) and the organized image inventory, recording for each image: current base image, target PQC base (`ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc`, `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal`, or a python/nodejs PQC variant), TLS stack classification (system OpenSSL vs. pure-Rust vs. Java/NSS), owning team, and switch-vs-exception decision.\n38\t- Stand up the aggregated compliance tracking view: all component team tracking tickets linked to the parent strategy, with per-image status roll-up.\n39\t- Drive the exception process: identify teams that cannot comply by September 15, remind teams weekly, and escalate to leadership for any image with neither a switch plan nor a filed exception (per the strategy's risk mitigation).\n40\t- Track the known exception candidates called out by the strategy: fms-guardrails-orchestrator (rustls+ring), the TrustyAI explainability service (Java/NSS, JDK 29 timeline September 2027), and the trainer data cache pending its TLS posture audit.\n41\t- Update matrix cells as the AIPCC base availability, guardrails feasibility, and trainer posture outcomes land.\n42\t\n43\t## Acceptance Criteria\n44\t\n45\t- The readiness matrix covers 100% of RHOAI 3.6 payload images with all classification fields populated.\n46\t- By September 15, 2026: zero unaccounted-for images — every image is rebuilt on a PQC-enabled base, has an in-progress switch with a linked team tracking ticket, or has a filed and acknowledged OCP exception in OCPEXCEPT documenting the component's TLS stack, why it cannot negotiate ML-KEM, and a remediation plan.\n47\t- All component team tracking tickets are linked to the parent strategy, providing the aggregated compliance view for OCP 5.0.\n48\t- Weekly status reporting and the escalation path for teams without a plan operate through the deadline.\n49\t\n50\t## HLR Traceability\n51\t\n52\t- HLR-1 (P0): provides the per-image accounting that all runtime images use a PQC-enabled base image or are exception-covered.\n53\t- HLR-2 (P0): drives the exception process so teams unable to comply by September 15 file OCP exceptions with remediation plans (per-team filing itself is out of scope; each team files its own).\n54\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a7f46cb863dca2e5","agent_id":"a8e80f1fe8fe243da","session_id":"2c0c1fc8-055a-4722-8bad-6dca3da0d413","uuid":"8a21ed05-d01e-4f00-882c-e2c9a