{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-81814d6541af746a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8001-E010\n3\ttitle: PQC compliance verification CI scan across the RHOAI payload\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n5\tcomponent: CI/CD\n6\tteam: DevOps/Build\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tai_signals:\n10\t  change_specificity: 1\n11\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n12\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n13\t  existing_foundation: 1\n14\t  open_questions: 1\n15\t  external_dependency: 0\n16\t  human_process_gates: 0\n17\t  repo_access: 1\n18\t  architecture_claims: 1\n19\timplementation_type: null\n20\tdependencies: []\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 5\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# PQC compliance verification CI scan across the RHOAI payload\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic builds the automated CI scan that measures the strategy's primary acceptance criterion: across the RHOAI 3.6 payload, every OpenSSL-backed container image rebuilt on a PQC-enabled base reports `DEFAULT:PQ` as its active crypto policy and exposes the ML-KEM algorithms in system OpenSSL. The scan produces the per-image pass/fail evidence that feeds the aggregated compliance view and closes the loop on the base image switch campaigns. It is distinct from the TLS-scanner CI owned by the central TLS profile strategy, which remains out of scope here.\n34\t\n35\t## Scope\n36\t\n37\t- Implement a CI scan job that, for each image in the RHOAI 3.6 payload list (sourced from the RHOAI-Build-Config CSV relatedImages inventory), executes `update-crypto-policies --show` and `openssl list -kem-algorithms` inside the container and records `DEFAULT:PQ` and the presence of ML-KEM-512, ML-KEM-768, and ML-KEM-1024.\n38\t- Produce a per-image pass/fail report aggregated across the payload, consumable by the compliance tracking view.\n39\t- Handle exemptions: images covered by filed and acknowledged OCP exceptions (non-OpenSSL TLS stacks such as rustls+ring and Java/NSS) are reported as exception-covered, not failed.\n40\t- Document the FIPS-mode caveat: on FIPS-mode clusters, FIPS policy deactivates PQC for this release — expected behavior, not a compliance failure for RHOAI 3.6 — so the scan does not flag FIPS-cluster results as failures.\n41\t\n42\t## Acceptance Criteria\n43\t\n44\t- The scan executes against the full RHOAI 3.6 payload image list and emits a per-image pass/fail for the `DEFAULT:PQ` policy check and the ML-KEM algorithm availability check.\n45\t- Images with acknowledged OCP exceptions are reported as exception-covered rather than failed, so the report distinguishes compliant, non-compliant, and exception-covered images.\n46\t- Scan results are consumed by the aggregated compliance view, satisfying the strategy's \"measured by automated CI scan across the RHOAI 3.6 payload\" clause.\n47\t- The FIPS-mode exemption is documented in the scan's reporting so FIPS-cluster behavior is not counted as a compliance failure.\n48\t\n49\t## HLR Traceability\n50\t\n51\t- HLR-1 (P0): provides the automated verification that all RHOAI runtime images use a PQC-enabled base image (or are exception-covered).\n52\t- HLR-3 (P0): the scan's per-image checks confirm the runtime-only switch took effect in the final image (policy active in the runtime container).\n53\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a7f46cb863dca2e5","agent_id":"a8e80f1fe8fe243da","session_id":"2c0c1fc8-055a-4722-8bad-6dca3da0d413","uuid":"63ed63e1-43ed-43fc-922d-a5bac4bc7ca8","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:15:38.038Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-81814d6541af746a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
