s E010's runtime policy checks. HLR-4 (ML-DSA) is explicitly deferred with justification — correct. Priority inheritance holds: E006/E007 (P0) gated by E001 (P0); E008/E009 (P1) and E011 (P1) gate nothing downstream and depend on P0 epics. No priority collapse — all P0 HLRs are bundled only with P0 work; the P1 migrations and the P1 performance measurement are in separately deferrable epics.\n34\t\n35\tMinor: E008/E009 cite HLR-2 (P0) in their HLR Traceability sections, but their substance is the Security NFR remediation path — the P0 compliance path for those components is exception filing, which E004 owns. Defensible (the migration is the alternative to needing the exception), but tracing them to the Security NFR (as E011 does for the Performance NFR) would be more precise.\n36\t\n37\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n38\tNo cycles. All five edges (E001→E006, E001→E007, E002→E008, E003→E009, E005→E011) appear in both the diagram (drawn dependency→dependent, matching the `graph TD` convention) and the target epics' frontmatter `dependencies`; the six isolated epics match empty dependency lists exactly. Every Investigation→Implementation edge is a proper true gate (`gated_by` set, `gate_failure_impact.action=remove` with a concrete fallback to the strategy's own OCPEXCEPT path). Parallelism is correctly preserved: the three investigations resolve distinct unknowns for distinct owners; E005/E006/E007 touch disjoint image families with disjoint gating; E004 builds from the existing RHOAI-Build-Config CSV rather than investigation output; E010 is config/tooling parallel to the work it verifies with its first full-payload execution AC-gated (Rule-25 pattern), not edge-gated. Critical path length 2 is appropriate for an S-sized strategy (heuristic: 1-2). No konflux-onboarding/repo-onboarding/license-validation epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 parallel-pair checks are N/A. No conditional branch files; conditional work is modeled via `gated_by`, correctly. No main-plan epic references a branch node.\n39\t\n40\tMinor: E011's scope says post-switch measurements use \"representative images from the switched families\" (plural), but E005 is its only dependency — measurements of RHEL9-ELS or python/nodejs family images would implicitly require E006/E007. The ACs only require \"representative RHOAI runtime images,\" so the epic is executable as gated; either restrict the sampling language to the ubi9-minimal family or note that other-family samples are opportunistic.\n41\t\n42\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n43\tThe three campaign epics (E005/E006/E007) share team/component but are split along the image-family axis — distinct current bases, distinct target bases, distinct external gating — which is the correct independent-planning split, not scope duplication: the image sets are disjoint and no surface is built twice. E004 (readiness matrix / tracking structure) and E010 (automated CI verification job) are distinct artifacts that integrate with each other (scan feeds the tracking view), not duplicate builds of the same data structure. E002/E008 and E003/E009 are per-component pairs with distinct component/team tuples. No epic is oversized: per-image Dockerfile work is team-owned per the strategy's own scope statement, and the epic-level work is family coordination plus shared build configuration. No epic bundles code implementation with content authoring.\n44\t\n45\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n46\tAll three Investigations genuinely change downstream epic existence: E001's outcome determines whether E006/E007 exist at all; E002 gates E008; E003 gates E009 — each has downstream dependents, so none is a misclassified Implementation. All Implementations produce concrete artifacts (matrix/tracking view, switched image families, TLS provider migrations, CI scan job, measurement report). Every epic with non-null `gated_by` (E006, E007, E008, E009) has both `gate_failure_impact.action` and `fallback_approach` populated, and every `gated_by` target appears