{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a2a0701d58a51563","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n3\tepic_count: 11\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8001 (Base image compliance — Post-quantum cryptography support)\n10\t\n11\t## Overview\n12\t\n13\tRHAISTRAT-8001 drives RHOAI's switch to PQC-enabled runtime base images for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0, with a September 15, 2026 deadline: every RHOAI runtime image either moves its final runtime stage to a PQC base image or its team files an OCP exception. The strategy states explicitly that it is a compliance coordination tracker — implementation work is owned by each component team, with teams linking their tracking tickets to the parent strategy, and per-team OCP exception filing is out of scope. The strategy-level work decomposed here is therefore: confirming the unconfirmed PQC base image dependencies (AIPCC), building and driving the compliance tracking structure, coordinating the per-family base image switch campaigns, verifying compliance via an automated CI scan, remediating the components a base image swap cannot fix (pure-Rust and Java/NSS TLS stacks), and measuring the TLS handshake performance regression.\n14\t\n15\tPQC enablement is runtime only (final stage of the Dockerfile/Containerfile); the two primary PQC base images (`ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc`, `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal`) are confirmed available, while python-311/312/312-minimal and nodejs-22 PQC variants are unconfirmed (AIPCC spike RHOAIENG-7195) and RHEL9-ELS adoption is gated by the AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase (AIPCC-7258).\n16\t\n17\tStaff Engineer / SME Input section is empty (template placeholder only) — no overrides to log.\n18\t\n19\t## Epic List\n20\t\n21\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n22\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E001 | Confirm AIPCC PQC base image variant availability and RHEL 9.8 rebase commitment | Investigation | AIPCC | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E002 | Investigate ML-KEM TLS migration feasibility for fms-guardrails-orchestrator | Investigation | AI Guardrails | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E003 | Audit trainer data cache Rust TLS posture | Investigation | Training | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E004 | PQC Readiness Matrix and aggregated compliance tracking | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E005 | Switch ubi9-minimal-based runtime images to ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E006 | Switch RHEL9-ELS-based runtime images to rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E007 | Switch python/nodejs-based runtime images to PQC base image variants | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E008 | Migrate fms-guardrails-orchestrator TLS stack to an ML-KEM-capable provider | Implementation | AI Guardrails | P1 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E009 | Migrate trainer data cache TLS stack to an ML-KEM-capable provider (conditional) | Implementation | Training | P1 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E010 | PQC compliance verification CI scan across the RHOAI payload | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E011 | TLS handshake latency regression threshold and measurement | Implementation | Performance | P1 |\n34\t\n35\t## Dependency DAG\n36\t\n37\t```mermaid\n38\tgraph TD\n39\t    E001[\"E001 AIPCC PQC base availability (Investigation)\"] --> E006[\"E006 RHEL9-ELS family switch\"]\n40\t    E001 --> E007[\"E007 python/nodejs family switch\"]\n41\t    E002[\"E002 guardrails ML-KEM feasibility (Investigation)\"] --> E008[\"E008 guardrails TLS migration\"]\n42\t    E003[\"E003 trainer data cache TLS audit (Investigation)\"] --> E009[\"E009 trainer data cache TLS migration (conditional)\"]\n43\t    E005[\"E005 ubi9-minimal family switch\"] --> E011[\"E011 TLS handshake latency measurement\"]\n44\t    E004[\"E004 PQC Readiness Matrix & tracking\"]\n45\t    E010[\"E010 PQC compliance CI scan\"]\n46\t```\n47\t\n48\t## DAG Justification\n49\t\n50\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n51\t|------|------|-----------|\n52\t| E001 → E006 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Whether the ~34 RHEL9-ELS-based images can adopt `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal` depends on the AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase committed date. True gate: if the rebase slips past the deadline, the campaign is removed and affected teams file OCP exceptions (`gate_failure_impact.action=remove`). |\n53\t| E001 → E007 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The target base for the ~11 python/nodejs-based images is TBD pending AIPCC PQC variants. True gate: if no variant is committed in time, the campaign is removed and affected teams (including the dashboard, which is largely behind a Go proxy) file OCP exceptions (`gate_failure_impact.action=remove`). |\n54\t| E002 → E008 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The guardrails TLS migration only exists if the feasibility spike (RHOAIENG-7452) finds an ML-KEM-capable rustls provider path viable within the deadline. True gate: if infeasible, the migration epic is removed and the team files an OCP exception with the migration as the remediation plan (`gate_failure_impact.action=remove`). |\n55\t| E003 → E009 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The trainer data cache migration is conditional on the TLS posture audit (RHOAIENG-7196): if the cache is OpenSSL/native-tls backed, the epic is removed (covered by the base image switch); if pure-Rust and not remediable in time, the team files an OCP exception (`gate_failure_impact.action=remove`). |\n56\t| E005 → E011 | Rule 13 (implementation producing artifact another epic builds on) | The PQC-side handshake latency measurements require rebuilt PQC runtime images; baseline capture on non-PQC images proceeds independently in parallel. |\n57\t| E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 ∥ E004 ∥ E005 ∥ E010 (no edges) | Rules 14/15 (independent work in parallel) | The three investigations resolve distinct unknowns for distinct owners. The readiness matrix builds from the existing RHOAI-Build-Config CSV inventory (a public, existing artifact), not from investigation output. The ubi9-minimal family switch uses a confirmed-available base image. The CI scan is built and integrated independently; its first full-payload execution is AC-gated on switched images (Rule 10 pattern — config/tooling parallel to the work it verifies, no DAG edge). |\n58\t| E005 ∥ E006 ∥ E007 (no edges between campaigns) | Rule 14/15 | The three switch campaigns touch disjoint image families with disjoint base images and disjoint gating; E006/E007 are gated only by E001. |\n59\t| No docs-authoring epic | Rule 11 n/a | Strategy `Documentation Support` is \"Pending human review\", not Yes; no substantive content authoring is in scope. Incidental doc touch-ups fold into the originating epics. |\n60\t\n61\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n62\t\n63\t| HLR | Priority | Epics | Coverage notes |\n64\t|-----|----------|-------|----------------|\n65\t| HLR-1: All RHOAI runtime images use a PQC-enabled base image | P0 | E005, E006, E007 (switch campaigns), E010 (automated verification), E008/E009 (non-OpenSSL components remediated via TLS migration) | Per-image Dockerfile changes are executed by component teams via tracking tickets linked to the parent strategy (per the strategy's own scope statement); the campaign epics coordinate, update shared build configuration, and are accountable for family completion. |\n66\t| HLR-2: Teams unable to comply by September 15 file an OCP exception with remediation plan | P0 | E004 (drives exception process, tracks zero-unaccounted-images), informed by E001/E002/E003 outcomes | Per-team exception filing itself is explicitly out of scope (each team files its own via OCPEXCEPT); E004 ensures the process is exercised and every image is accounted for. |\n67\t| HLR-3: PQC enablement is runtime only — only the final stage changes, no builder image change | P0 | E005, E006, E007 (scope constraint and acceptance criteria) | Folded into each campaign epic as an explicit scope boundary and pass/fail criterion. |\n68\t| HLR-4: PQC-signed X.509 certificates (ML-DSA) | Deferred | — | Explicitly deferred by the strategy; no epic. |\n69\t\n70\t## Component Mapping Notes\n71\t\n72\tCanonical component names from `.context/rhai-components.txt` are used verbatim. Non-obvious mappings:\n73\t\n74\t- **E005/E006/E007 → `Build and Release`**: these campaigns span many strategy components (Go operators and platform components; notebooks, workbenches, training runtimes, mlserver; training variants, lmes-job, feature-server, dashboard). The strategy assigns base image pipeline coordination to the DevOps/Build team, so `Build and Release` is the single most-relevant canonical name; the component span is noted in each epic's scope.\n75\t- **E004 → `Build and Release`**: coordination/tracking artifact owned by the same DevOps/Build team.\n76\t- **E010 → `CI/CD`**: the automated payload-wide compliance scan is a CI deliverable.\n77\t- **E001 → `AIPCC Productization`**: the AIPCC team owns delivery/confirmation of the PQC base image variants and the RHEL 9.8 rebase.\n78\t- **E002/E008 → `AI Guardrails`**: fms-guardrails-orchestrator (architecture context confirms rustls+ring, non-OpenSSL release image).\n79\t- **E003/E009 → `Training Kubeflow`**: trainer data cache (trainer component; architecture context covers the trainer operator, the data cache TLS posture is the unknown under audit).\n80\t- **E011 → `PerfScale`**: TLS handshake latency regression measurement (Performance team).\n81\t\n82\t## Health Warnings\n83\t\n84\t- **Performance NFR flagged NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT**: no numeric TLS handshake regression threshold is set; the strategy requires the PM + performance team lead to resolve it before Sprint 2 ends. Captured as a mandatory acceptance criterion on E011; if unresolved, the measurement cannot gate the release. External references: Chrome ~10% max handshake regression, PSASTRAT-7170.\n85\t- **NFR vs. technical approach tension (fms-guardrails-orchestrator)**: the Security NFR demands ML-KEM hybrid key exchange after migration, while the technical approach expects the component to file an OCP exception (rustls+ring cannot inherit PQC from a base image; JDK-style long timelines). Resolved structurally: a feasibility investigation gates a migration epic whose fallback is the exception path. Same pattern for trainer data cache (posture unknown).\n86\t- **Effort estimate vs. scope**: the strategy is S-sized as a coordination tracker, but its scope spans ~153 images across all RHOAI component teams. The S estimate reflects strategy-level coordination effort only; total platform effort is carried by team-owned tracking tickets. The decomposition mirrors this: campaign epics coordinate rather than centrally implement.\n87\t- **FIPS + PQC incompatibility**: on FIPS-mode clusters, FIPS policy deactivates PQC for this release — expected behavior, not a compliance failure for RHOAI 3.6. The CI scan (E010) and campaign ACs carry this exemption explicitly to avoid false failures.\n88\t- **Deadline risk**: September 15, 2026 is an external OCP 5.0 compliance deadline with no slack; the escalation path (weekly reminders, leadership escalation per the strategy's risk mitigation) is captured in E004. AIPCC commitment uncertainty is escalated per the strategy's risk mitigation if no committed date exists by early September.\n89\t- **Ambiguity — \"equivalent\" base images**: HLR-1 allows \"or equivalent\" PQC bases; the decomposition uses the two confirmed images from the strategy and treats any other base as an investigation outcome, not an implementation decision made here.\n90\t\n91\t## Tiered Delivery\n92\t\n93\tThe AIPCC dependencies (python/nodejs PQC variants, RHEL 9.8 rebase) have uncertain timing, and partial value is deliverable without them — so the strategy is split into tiers:\n94\t\n95\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately)**: E004 (readiness matrix and tracking), E005 (~68 ubi9-minimal-based images on a confirmed-available base), E010 (compliance CI scan), E002/E003 investigations, and the baseline half of E011.\n96\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E006 and E007 (gated by AIPCC base availability/rebase commitment), E008 (gated by the guardrails feasibility outcome), E009 (gated by the trainer audit outcome), and the PQC-side measurements of E011.\n97\t\n98\tIf a Tier 2 gate fails, the affected component teams file OCP exceptions via OCPEXCEPT with documented remediation plans — the strategy's own fallback — and E004 accounts for those images as exception-covered rather than switched.\n99\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b497e7ecb7b91a47","agent_id":"a968e19c50823e180","session_id":"2c0c1fc8-055a-4722-8bad-6dca3da0d413","uuid":"a1d82575-e72c-4bc0-9a34-7bb580fbd619","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:19:50.728Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a2a0701d58a51563","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
