{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad8831c83b08d146","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8001-E002\n3\ttitle: Investigate ML-KEM TLS migration feasibility for fms-guardrails-orchestrator\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n5\tcomponent: AI Guardrails\n6\tteam: AI Guardrails\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tinvestigation_signals:\n10\t  question_specificity: 1\n11\t  source_accessibility: 1\n12\t  local_runnability: 1\n13\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n14\t  human_judgment_required: 0\n15\timplementation_type: null\n16\tdependencies: []\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: High\n19\tai_implementability_score: 3\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Investigate ML-KEM TLS migration feasibility for fms-guardrails-orchestrator\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tThis investigation determines whether the fms-guardrails-orchestrator service — whose TLS stack is rustls with the ring provider across its inbound HTTPS surface and downstream gRPC/REST clients, and which therefore cannot inherit PQC from a base image swap — can be migrated to an ML-KEM (FIPS 203) capable TLS configuration in time for the September 15, 2026 deadline. It tracks TLS migration spike RHOAIENG-7452. The outcome decides whether the guardrails TLS migration proceeds or the team files an OCP exception documenting the TLS stack, why it cannot negotiate ML-KEM, and the migration as the remediation plan.\n30\t\n31\t## Scope\n32\t\n33\t- Identify a rustls provider configuration (for example, an ML-KEM-capable crypto provider and the crate versions that enable X25519MLKEM768 hybrid key exchange) applicable to the orchestrator's dependency set: rustls, hyper-rustls, tokio-rustls, and tonic TLS.\n34\t- Verify, ideally with a local build and integration test, that the candidate configuration negotiates ML-KEM hybrid key exchange while remaining backwards-compatible with classic ECDHE TLS 1.3 clients.\n35\t- Estimate the migration effort (dependency and feature-flag changes, test coverage, review and release) against the September 15 deadline.\n36\t- Record a recommendation: migrate now, or file an OCP exception via OCPEXCEPT with the migration as the documented remediation plan.\n37\t- Hand findings to the exception filing if that path is chosen, so the filing states the component's TLS stack and why it cannot negotiate ML-KEM, as the strategy's acceptance criteria require.\n38\t\n39\t## Acceptance Criteria\n40\t\n41\t- A documented answer to: which provider/library configuration (with versions) enables ML-KEM hybrid key exchange for the orchestrator's inbound TLS and downstream TLS clients, or a documented conclusion that none is viable.\n42\t- A verified demonstration (local build/integration test) of X25519MLKEM768 hybrid negotiation with a PQC-capable peer and continued interoperability with a non-PQC TLS 1.3 client, where a viable configuration exists.\n43\t- An effort estimate and timeline for the migration compared against the September 15, 2026 deadline.\n44\t- A recorded recommendation (migrate vs. OCP exception with remediation plan) usable directly for the compliance decision.\n45\t\n46\t## HLR Traceability\n47\t\n48\t- HLR-2 (P0): the feasibility outcome is the decision input for the guardrails team's exception filing (or avoidance of one) by September 15.\n49\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b9ccd10a634b7b2f","agent_id":"a0dd64499fae42080","session_id":"2c0c1fc8-055a-4722-8bad-6dca3da0d413","uuid":"f50d4569-9279-42c5-abc7-3ea8fb8a9c9f","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:22:01.777Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad8831c83b08d146","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
