he September 15, 2026 deadline. Per-image Dockerfile changes are executed by the owning component teams through their tracking tickets linked to the parent strategy; this epic owns family coordination, shared build configuration updates, and accountability for family completion.\n40\t\n41\t## Scope\n42\t\n43\t- Coordinate the final-runtime-stage base switch to the confirmed PQC variant for each of the python-311, python-312, python-312-minimal, and nodejs-22 based images in the RHOAI 3.6 payload, via component team tracking tickets.\n44\t- Update shared build configuration and pipeline base image references once variant names/locations are confirmed.\n45\t- Enforce the runtime-only constraint: only the final stage of each Containerfile changes; no builder image changes.\n46\t- Roll up per-image switch status into the compliance tracking view; for any image without a viable variant in time, trigger the OCP exception path via OCPEXCEPT with a documented remediation plan. The dashboard image (largely behind a Go proxy) is the strategy's named fallback candidate for the exception route.\n47\t\n48\t## Acceptance Criteria\n49\t\n50\t- Each python/nodejs-based runtime image in the RHOAI 3.6 payload is rebuilt with its final runtime stage based on the confirmed PQC variant, or carries a filed OCP exception.\n51\t- For each switched image: `update-crypto-policies --show` executed inside the container reports `DEFAULT:PQ`, and `openssl list -kem-algorithms` includes ML-KEM-512, ML-KEM-768, and ML-KEM-1024 (FIPS-mode clusters are exempt for RHOAI 3.6).\n52\t- Only the final runtime stage of each Containerfile changes; no builder image is changed.\n53\t- Build pipelines are green for all rebuilt images.\n54\t- Base image reference changes are revertible: the previous base reference is retained so any image can be rolled back.\n55\t\n56\t## HLR Traceability\n57\t\n58\t- HLR-1 (P0): delivers the PQC-enabled base image switch for the python/nodejs-based family.\n59\t- HLR-3 (P0): 