{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a341d1aec9481cf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8001-E008\n3\ttitle: Migrate fms-guardrails-orchestrator TLS stack to an ML-KEM-capable provider\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n5\tcomponent: AI Guardrails\n6\tteam: AI Guardrails\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8001-E002\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8001-E002\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: remove\n14\t  fallback_approach: If the feasibility investigation finds no viable ML-KEM-capable\n15\t    rustls configuration within the deadline, the guardrails team files an OCP exception\n16\t    via OCPEXCEPT documenting the rustls+ring TLS stack, why it cannot negotiate ML-KEM,\n17\t    and the migration as the remediation plan.\n18\tai_signals:\n19\t  change_specificity: 0\n20\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n21\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n22\t  existing_foundation: 1\n23\t  open_questions: -1\n24\t  external_dependency: 0\n25\t  human_process_gates: 0\n26\t  repo_access: 1\n27\t  architecture_claims: 1\n28\timplementation_type: null\n29\tinvestigation_signals: null\n30\tai_implementability: High\n31\tai_implementability_score: 3\n32\tjira_key: null\n33\tbranch: null\n34\t---\n35\t# Migrate fms-guardrails-orchestrator TLS stack to an ML-KEM-capable provider\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tThis epic migrates the fms-guardrails-orchestrator service's TLS stack from rustls with the ring provider — which cannot inherit PQC from a base image swap and cannot negotiate ML-KEM — to an ML-KEM (FIPS 203) capable configuration, so the service supports X25519MLKEM768 hybrid key exchange per the strategy's security NFR. It proceeds only if the feasibility investigation recommends migration within the deadline; otherwise the team files an OCP exception with this migration as the documented remediation plan.\n40\t\n41\t## Scope\n42\t\n43\t- Swap the crypto provider selection across the orchestrator's TLS dependency set (rustls, hyper-rustls, tokio-rustls, tonic TLS) to the ML-KEM-capable configuration identified by the feasibility investigation, including any required crate version updates.\n44\t- Preserve the existing TLS configuration surface: optional server TLS and mTLS via CLI flags/environment variables, and per-service TLS configuration for downstream gRPC/REST clients.\n45\t- Extend integration tests to cover ML-KEM hybrid negotiation and classic ECDHE fallback on both inbound and outbound connections.\n46\t- Keep the base image switch for platform consistency (the service's release image does not include OpenSSL; the migration, not the base, provides ML-KEM).\n47\t\n48\t## Acceptance Criteria\n49\t\n50\t- The orchestrator negotiates X25519MLKEM768 hybrid key exchange with PQC-capable peers on its inbound API surface, verified by an integration test.\n51\t- Backwards compatibility: non-PQC TLS 1.3 clients continue to connect via classic ECDHE (opportunistic hybrid negotiation, per the strategy's NFR).\n52\t- No change to HA characteristics: same deployment shape and replica behavior (single service, no additional replicas required).\n53\t- Rollback plan: the provider selection is revertible to the previous pinned configuration, and the prior release remains deployable.\n54\t- Build pipeline green for the rebuilt image.\n55\t\n56\t## HLR Traceability\n57\t\n58\t- HLR-1 (indirect): extends PQC coverage to a component whose release image cannot inherit ML-KEM from a base image swap; no direct HLR — this epic implements the Security NFR (ML-KEM (FIPS 203) hybrid key exchange for non-Go components after migration). The P0 compliance path for this component is the OCP exception filing driven by the readiness matrix and compliance tracking work; this migration is the remediation that makes an exception unnecessary, with the exception as the fallback if it cannot land by September 15.\n59\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b9ccd10a634b7b2